<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>nish/crypto/ed25519 implements Ed25519 as RFC 8032 Section 5.1 defines it,
with an empty context and no prehash. The private key is the 32-octet seed,
which the module takes as a <tt>Secret</tt>. Key generation and signing return
<tt>u8[] | null</tt>, null for a seed of the wrong length; verification returns a
boolean.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="interface">
      <name>Interface</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-interface"><![CDATA[
// module: nish/crypto/ed25519
export const ed25519PublicKey = (seed: Secret<u8[]>): u8[] | null
export const ed25519Sign = (seed: Secret<u8[]>, msg: u8[]): u8[] | null
export const ed25519Verify = (pub: u8[], msg: u8[], sig: u8[]): boolean
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
    <section anchor="ed25519">
      <name>Ed25519</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-PURE">
        <t>For Ed25519, dom2(f,c) is the empty string.
<xref section="5.1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        <t>The context (if present at all) <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be empty.
<xref section="5.1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: nish implements PureEdDSA Ed25519 only: no function takes a context, so it is always empty</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="key-generation">
      <name>Key Generation</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-PUB-32">
        <t>The 32-byte public key is generated by the following steps.
<xref section="5.1.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-PUB-ENCODE">
        <t>The public key A is the encoding of the point [s]B.
<xref section="5.1.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="sign">
      <name>Sign</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-SIG-FORM">
        <t>Form the signature of the concatenation of R (32 octets) and the little-endian encoding of S (32 octets; the three most significant bits of the final octet are always zero).
<xref section="5.1.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="verify">
      <name>Verify</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-VERIFY-DECODE">
        <t>If any of the decodings fail (including S being out of range), the signature is invalid.
<xref section="5.1.7" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-VERIFY-EQ">
        <t>Check the group equation [8][S]B = [8]R + [8][k]A'.
<xref section="5.1.7" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations">
      <name>Security Considerations</name>
      <blockquote>
        <t>In particular, unless the underlying protocol does not require it, the receiver <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> buffer the entire message (or enough information to reconstruct it, e.g., compressed or encrypted version) to be verified.
<xref section="8.7" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8032"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: an obligation on the caller: ed25519Verify takes the whole message, and what the receiver does with it is outside the module</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="out-of-scope">
      <name>Out of Scope</name>
      <t>Ed25519ctx and Ed25519ph (RFC 8032 Section 5.1) and Ed448 and Ed448ph
(Section 5.2) are not exported, so their sections and their test vectors
(Sections 7.2 to 7.5) are not part of this profile.</t>
      <t>Three statements of RFC 8032 are not functional and hold no BCP 14 keyword,
so they are recorded here, not excluded:</t>
      <ul spacing="normal">
        <li>
          <t>Section 5.1.5: the private key is "cryptographically secure random data".
The seed is the caller's input, and its randomness is not observable by a
test.</t>
        </li>
        <li>
          <t>Section 8.1: "the modulo p arithmetic must not use any data-dependent
branches". Timing is not observable by a functional test. nish checks the
field arithmetic Ed25519 shares with X25519 on the emitted machine code
(tests/cases/ct_asm_x25519.ts) and records the rest in
docs/security/crypto-ecc.md (ECC-5).</t>
        </li>
        <li>
          <t>Section 8.1: "secrecy of the private key is fundamental". nish takes the
seed as a <tt>Secret</tt> and pins the wipes of its hash, the nonce and the
scratch under opt -O2 (tests/wipes/crypto.js, tests/run.js), recorded in
docs/security/crypto-ecc.md.</t>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <t>Section 7.1's public keys and signatures are vectors. Its verifications are
not: a row expecting <tt>true</tt> has to cover a requirement cited to Section 7.1
that states the result, and the section states none. The properties hold
verification instead, and tests/link/crypto_ed25519/rfc.ts verifies all
five Section 7.1 tests against the printed signatures.</t>
      <t>Out of scope: ED25519_SEED_SIZE, an <tt>i32</tt> constant a vector or property cannot call; every property draws a 32-octet seed</t>
      <t>Out of scope: ED25519_PUBLIC_KEY_SIZE, an <tt>i32</tt> constant a vector or property cannot call; P-PUB-32 holds the 32-byte public key</t>
      <t>Out of scope: ED25519_SIGNATURE_SIZE, an <tt>i32</tt> constant a vector or property cannot call; P-SIG-FORM holds the 64-octet signature</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="properties">
      <name>Properties</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-PUB-32 covers R-PUB-32 — any 32-octet seed gives a 32-byte public key
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */) =>
  unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)).length === 32
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-SIG-FORM covers R-SIG-FORM — a signature is 64 octets and the three most significant bits of its final octet are zero
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..130 */) =>
  { const sig = unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg)); return sig.length === 64 && bitAnd(sig.slice(63, 64), fill(224, 1)) === zeros(1); }
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ROUNDTRIP covers R-VERIFY-EQ — a signature verifies under the seed's public key
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..1023 */) =>
  ed25519Verify(unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)), msg, unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg))) === true
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAMPER-SIG covers R-VERIFY-EQ — flipping any one bit of the signature makes it invalid
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..100 */, bit: i32 /* 0..511 */) =>
  ed25519Verify(unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)), msg, flipBit(unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg)), bit)) === false
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAMPER-MSG covers R-VERIFY-EQ — flipping any one bit of the message makes the signature invalid
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 1..100 */, bit: i32 /* 0..799 */) =>
  ed25519Verify(unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)), flipBit(msg, bit), unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg))) === false
where bit < 8 * msg.length
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-S-RANGE covers R-VERIFY-DECODE — setting any of the three top bits of S puts it out of range, and the signature is invalid
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..100 */, bit: i32 /* 509..511 */) =>
  ed25519Verify(unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)), msg, flipBit(unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg)), bit)) === false
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-SIZES covers R-VERIFY-DECODE — a public key other than 32 octets or a signature other than 64 is invalid
forall (seed: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..40 */, cut: i32 /* 1..32 */) =>
  { const pub = unwrap(ed25519PublicKey(seed)); const sig = unwrap(ed25519Sign(seed, msg)); return ed25519Verify(pub.slice(0, 32 - cut), msg, sig) === false && ed25519Verify(pub, msg, sig.slice(0, 64 - cut)) === false; }
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
